---
import Post from '../../layouts/Post.astro';
import { getPost } from '../../data/posts';

const post = getPost('repair-or-replace-water-heater');

const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the 50% rule for water heaters?',
    a: 'If the repair quote comes to more than half the installed cost of a comparable new unit, you replace instead. It is a rule of thumb rather than a law of physics, but it stops people spending hundreds on a tank that has two winters left in it.' },
  { q: 'Is it worth repairing a 12-year-old water heater?',
    a: 'Only if the repair is genuinely small — a thermocouple, a relief valve, a thermostat. Anything in the hundreds on a twelve-year-old tank is money you will not get back, because the tank itself is the part that is aging and that part cannot be replaced.' },
  { q: 'Can a leaking water heater be repaired?',
    a: 'It depends where the leak is. Fittings, nipples, the drain valve and the T&amp;P relief valve are all repairable. A leak coming from the body of the tank is not — the steel has corroded through from the inside and the only fix is a new heater.' },
  { q: 'Does a new water heater use less gas than my old one?',
    a: 'Usually a little, yes. Newer tanks have better insulation and cleaner burners, and a tank free of sediment transfers heat properly. Do not expect a dramatic drop on a like-for-like tank swap, though — the larger efficiency gains come from heat pump or condensing tankless units.' },
  { q: 'How long does a replacement take compared with a repair?',
    a: 'Most repairs are an hour or two. A straight tank-for-tank replacement is usually a half day, including draining the old unit, bringing the strapping, pan and relief discharge up to standard, and filling and testing the new one.' },
];
---
<Post
  image="/images/water-heater-repair-rocklin-plumber-diagnosing-unit.webp"
  imageAlt="Plumber weighing up whether a water heater is worth repairing in Rocklin, California"
  imageW={1600}
  imageH={2844}
  imageCaption="The age of the unit and the size of the repair bill decide this, not a rule of thumb."
  title={post.title}
  description="Use the 50% rule, sensible age thresholds and a simple fault table to decide whether your water heater is worth repairing or whether it is time to replace."
  path={`/blog/${post.slug}`}
  published={post.published}
  excerpt={post.excerpt}
  faqs={faqs}
  ctaHeading="Want Both Numbers Before You Decide?"
  ctaText="We will quote the repair and the replacement on the same visit, in writing, and tell you which one we would pick if it were our own house."
>
  <p>
    Nobody plans this decision. It arrives on a Tuesday morning with a cold shower, or on a
    Saturday with a damp patch creeping out from under the tank. And because you are deciding
    under pressure, it is easy to either throw good money at a dying heater or get talked into a
    replacement you did not need.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only three things actually matter: how old the unit is, what has failed, and what the repair
    costs relative to a new install. Work through those in order and the answer is usually
    obvious within a couple of minutes.
  </p>

  <h2>The 50% Rule</h2>
  <p>
    The trade shorthand is simple. <strong>If the repair costs more than half of what a
    comparable new unit would cost installed, replace it.</strong> Spend a couple of hundred
    dollars on a heater that would cost two thousand to replace and you have bought cheap time.
    Spend most of that two thousand on the old unit and you have paid nearly new-unit money with
    none of the warranty and none of the remaining service life.
  </p>
  <p>
    The rule works because of the way water heaters fail. The expensive, failure-prone components
    tend to go late in life, and they tend to go in clusters. A gas control valve dying at year
    twelve is rarely the last thing that unit will ask for. So tighten the rule as the heater
    ages: at year five, half the replacement cost is a fair ceiling; at year twelve, we would use
    something closer to a third.
  </p>

  <h2>Age Does Most of the Deciding</h2>
  <p>
    A glass-lined steel tank is a consumable. Water sits in it above 120&deg;F for a decade while
    the anode rod slowly sacrifices itself to protect the steel. Once that rod is used up, the
    tank itself starts corroding from the inside, and no repair reverses that.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>0&ndash;6 years:</strong> repair, almost without exception. The unit may still be
      covered by a manufacturer parts warranty, so ask &mdash; you may only be paying labor.</li>
    <li><strong>7&ndash;10 years:</strong> repair small faults, think hard about big ones. This is
      where the 50% rule earns its keep.</li>
    <li><strong>11&ndash;13 years:</strong> replace unless the fix is trivial. You are at or past
      the typical service life for a standard tank in a hard-water area.</li>
    <li><strong>14 years and up:</strong> replace. Every month beyond this is borrowed time, and
      the failure mode at the end is water on the floor, not a polite error code.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">
    Those brackets reflect the general industry range for conventional tanks and what we see
    locally. A well-maintained unit can comfortably beat them; a neglected one often will not.
  </p>

  <h2>Cheap Faults vs Terminal Faults</h2>
  <p>
    Two heaters can both be broken and be in completely different situations. What failed matters
    as much as how old the unit is.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="note" style="caption-side:bottom;text-align:left;padding:10px 16px">
        Verdicts assume a standard garage or utility-closet install. Your written estimate is
        always confirmed on site before any work starts.
      </caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">What failed</th><th scope="col">Verdict</th><th scope="col">Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Thermocouple or pilot assembly</td><td>Cheap repair</td><td>A wear part. Replacing it is routine on any age of unit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heating element or thermostat (electric)</td><td>Cheap repair</td><td>Designed to be replaced. Usually done in a single visit.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>T&amp;P relief valve weeping</td><td>Cheap repair</td><td>Often a safety valve doing its job, or a tired valve past its best.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Sediment noise and slow recovery</td><td>Maintenance</td><td>A proper flush frequently restores performance and buys years.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Broken dip tube</td><td>Moderate repair</td><td>Worth doing on a younger tank, marginal value past year ten.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Gas control valve</td><td>Depends on age</td><td>The classic 50%-rule call. Sensible at year six, poor value at year twelve.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Tankless heat exchanger</td><td>Depends on age</td><td>The most expensive part in the unit. Compare it against a whole new tankless install.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Leak from the body of the tank</td><td>Terminal</td><td>Internal corrosion. There is nothing to repair &mdash; the tank is the failure.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rust through the outer jacket or base ring</td><td>Terminal</td><td>By the time corrosion shows on the outside, the inside is long gone.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>A Decision Table You Can Use in Five Minutes</h2>
  <p>
    Find your age bracket, find the column that matches the quote you were given, and read off
    the answer. Treat a small fault as one priced like a thermocouple or an element, a mid-cost
    fault as one priced like a gas control valve, and a major fault as anything approaching the
    cost of a new unit.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Age of unit</th><th scope="col">Small fault</th><th scope="col">Mid-cost fault</th><th scope="col">Major fault or tank leak</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Under 6 years</td><td>Repair</td><td>Repair &mdash; check the warranty first</td><td>Repair unless the tank itself is leaking</td></tr>
        <tr><td>6&ndash;10 years</td><td>Repair</td><td>Apply the 50% rule</td><td>Replace</td></tr>
        <tr><td>11&ndash;13 years</td><td>Repair, and start budgeting</td><td>Replace</td><td>Replace</td></tr>
        <tr><td>14 years or more</td><td>Repair only to buy time</td><td>Replace</td><td>Replace</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Four Things People Forget</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Where the heater lives changes the maths</strong> A tank in a finished laundry room or an upstairs closet carries far more downside than one sitting on a garage slab near a drain. Replace earlier where a failure would be expensive.</li>
    <li><strong>A repair leaves you no paperwork</strong> A permitted replacement gives you documentation that helps at resale and on an insurance claim. A repair gives you an invoice and nothing else.</li>
    <li><strong>The unit may have been the wrong size all along</strong> If your household has been running out of hot water for years, a repair simply restores the problem. A replacement is the moment to size it properly.</li>
    <li><strong>Code upgrades ride along with a replacement</strong> Seismic strapping, relief-valve discharge, a drain pan and thermal expansion control where required all get brought up to current standard as part of a permitted swap.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The One Case Where There Is No Decision</h2>
  <p>
    If water is pooling under the tank and it is not coming from a fitting, the relief discharge
    or condensation on the cold line, the inner tank has corroded through. There is no patch, no
    weld and no sealant that makes that safe or durable. Shut the cold inlet valve on top of the
    heater, turn the gas control to off or switch off the breaker, and get it replaced. Everything
    else on this page is a judgment call; that one is not.
  </p>

  <h2>How We Handle a Borderline Call</h2>
  <p>
    When a unit sits right on the line we quote both options on the same visit and hand you the
    two numbers together. You get an honest read on remaining life with it &mdash; not vague
    warnings that it could go any day, but a real assessment of the tank, the state of the anode
    rod, and how much sediment came out of the drain valve while we were there.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the fix is sensible, we do the <a href="/">repair</a> and you keep your money. If it is
    not, we show you the <a href="/water-heater-replacement-rocklin">replacement</a> numbers,
    permit included, and let you decide without a countdown clock. A repair is the easier sale for
    us on the day, which is precisely why you should trust us when we tell you not to bother.
  </p>
</Post>
